## Problem

Shared shopping lists fail in the two places they are actually used. The first is
the planning table: you decide on meals for the week somewhere else — a recipe
site, a note, a conversation — and then retype the ingredients into a list by
hand. The second is the shop itself, where the aisles that need the list most are
the ones with no usable mobile signal, and a list that needs a round-trip to
render is a list you can't tick off.

## Approach

One app that accepts both entry points — add items directly, or pull a recipe in
and push its ingredients onto a list — and treats the offline case as the normal
case rather than an error path. Recipes can be typed, or imported by pasting a
link: the page is fetched, an LLM extracts ingredients and method, and the result
is always shown for editing before anything is saved. The web app installs as a
PWA and registers an Android share target at `/share`, so sharing a recipe URL
from a browser lands directly in the import flow.

## Architecture

A Bun-workspace monorepo with three packages: `web`, `api`, and a `types` package
holding the interfaces both sides compile against.

`web` is React 19 on Vite — Tailwind and Radix for UI, React Query for server
state, react-hook-form with Zod for forms, and `vite-plugin-pwa` driving the
service worker and manifest.

`api` is Hono served by `Bun.serve`, laid out as domain / infrastructure /
interfaces with a DI container in `dependencies/`. Domain services
(`ListService`, `RecipeService`, `RecipeImportService`, `NotificationService`,
`DailyReminderScheduler`) depend on repository interfaces; the concrete
`Mongo*Repository` classes, the MinIO bucket store, the fal.ai LLM and image
clients, and the `WebPushSender` all live behind those interfaces in
`infrastructure/`. Images go to MinIO, everything else to MongoDB. The API also
exposes `/api/metrics` for Prometheus, instrumented per route.

Authentication is not in this repo. A separate service — [kivo](/projects/kivo/) —
issues access and refresh tokens, and the web client retries once through a
transparent refresh when a request comes back `401`.

## Decisions and trade-offs

**Offline writes are an outbox of intents, not a cached response.** The
alternative — optimistic UI plus a retry on the in-flight request — dies the
moment the tab is closed, which is exactly what happens when a phone goes in a
pocket. Instead every mutation is recorded as a typed intent (`item.add`,
`item.toggle`, `item.rename`, `item.quantity`, …) in an IndexedDB outbox. Reads
fold the pending intents over whatever the server last returned, so the list you
see is the list you'll get. On reconnect the drainer replays them in order, and
deliberately *discards* — rather than retries — an intent that comes back `404`
or `409`: the item was deleted or already changed by someone else, and replaying
it would resurrect it. The cost is that this is last-writer-wins, not a CRDT;
for a household shopping list that is the right amount of machinery.

**Auth is its own service, not a module.** Several apps in this account share the
same users, so token issuing and rotation belong in one place. The price is an
extra network hop on every authorised request and a second service to deploy; the
win is one place to reason about sessions and one secret to rotate.

**The LLM is behind an explicit action, and its output is never trusted
silently.** Recipe import is a deliberate "paste a link" step and the extracted
ingredients are rendered into an editable form before save. Auto-importing on
paste would have been fewer taps and would have quietly written model
hallucinations into someone's shopping list.

## Outcome

Runs at [shoppingo.imapps.uk](https://shoppingo.imapps.uk) on a self-hosted
Dokploy host, behind Traefik and a Cloudflare tunnel, sharing that host's
MongoDB and MinIO over the internal Docker network. Every PR runs Biome, the API
and web unit suites and a Playwright e2e run including visual regression;
merges to `main` cut a semantic-release version, publish images and deploy.

It began in late 2023 as a much smaller DynamoDB-backed prototype and was rebuilt
from February 2025 onto the current MongoDB/Hono shape — the offline outbox and
the recipe pipeline are all post-rebuild. The next thing I'd change is the
conflict story: discarding a `409` is honest but silent, and the sync badge
should say what it dropped.
